WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.660
SPEAKER: Mute Findings is a
powerful volume management tool

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.730
within Security Command Center.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.010
It allows you to
automatically silence

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.830
findings based on
criteria you specify,

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.250
or manually silence
individual findings.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.850
This feature is relevant for
many potential use cases.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.330
For example, assets within
non-production environments

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.750
where some stricter requirements
may not be applicable,

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.600
recommendations to use
customer-managed encryption

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.290
keys in projects that don't
contain critical data,

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.920
when granting broad access on
a datastore which intentionally

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.930
is open to the public
and disseminates

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.020
public information,
or for findings

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.060
not relevant to
your organization

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.000
based on your
company's policies.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.300
Muted findings don't appear by
default in the Security Command

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.380
Center dashboard.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.450
However, they
continue to be logged

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.070
and can be accessed as needed.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.400
There are two ways
you can mute findings.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.519
You can create mute rules that
automatically mute findings

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.420
as they are generated, or you
can choose to mute and unmute

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.100
individual findings.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.620
Let's see this
feature in action.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.410
Currently we are located at
the Security Command Center

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.990
Findings tab for a
specific project.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.090
We'll focus on a
category of findings

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.880
that are generated when
bucket logging is disabled.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.250
Suppose we want to silence
findings within this category.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.630
We can use the Mute Findings
feature to hide them.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.630
As you can see, we can
select individual findings

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.720
to mute and unmute using
the Mute Options menu,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.850
but you can also
create a mute rule

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.920
to automatically mute
findings as they are

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.440
generated based on a criteria.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.960
First we'll give
the mute rule an ID.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.990
Note that this rule will apply
to all findings generated

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.490
within the resource
that we have selected.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.670
Now we'll set up the
filter by selecting

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:57.500
Category equal to
Bucket Logging Disabled.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.710
After clicking Apply, we can
preview any existing findings

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.780
that match this filter.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.500
Once this rule is implemented,
all existing and new findings

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.690
that exactly match the
filter will be hidden,

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:12.390
and the mute attribute for the
findings will be set to Muted.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.390
One thing to note--
if you are creating

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:18.230
mute rules with the gcloud tool
or Security Command Center API,

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.360
muting existing
and future findings

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.810
are two separate operations,
so be sure to check out

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.360
the documentation for details.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.900
After clicking Save, we'll
return to the Security Command

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.900
Center and review our findings.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.880
Our new mute rule
has taken effect,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.760
and we can no longer see any
findings in the Bucket Logging

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.470
Disabled category.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:42.110
As mentioned, mute rules
created in the Console

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.540
will mute both existing
and future findings

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.900
that match the filter.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.960
So let's create another
bucket to see how

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.310
future findings are handled.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:58.150
We're back.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.980
After creating a
new bucket, we can

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.500
see that no additional
findings in the Bucket Logging

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.430
Disabled category are visible.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:07.210
However, findings that
are muted are not deleted,

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.510
and new findings that match mute
rules continue to be logged.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.930
As such, they can be viewed
by selecting More Options

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.510
and choosing Include
Muted Findings.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.210
Now we can see the muted
findings, including

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:20.950
the additional
finding that was just

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.900
created for our new bucket.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.940
The Include Muted
Findings option

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.680
is useful when you
are trying to review

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.080
all findings for any
audit or compliance needs.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.400
Now if we would like to review
only the muted findings,

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.200
we can apply a filter--

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.660
Mute equals Muted.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:41.320
In these muted findings, we can
see when a finding was muted

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.950
and who muted the finding, which
we can use for audit purposes

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:46.360
at any time.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.850
We can also configure
any notifications

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.800
that we've set up
to exclude updates

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.070
from any new or existing
findings that are muted.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:57.800
Mute rules can be managed
from the Mute Settings page.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.920
On this page, we
can view our rules,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.230
edit them, and delete them.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.060
When we delete a
rule, it doesn't

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.950
unmute any findings
that were muted

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.290
in the past as a
result of this rule,

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:11.440
but future findings
will no longer be muted.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:12.790
Congratulations.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:14.890
You're now equipped to
use the Mute Findings

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.279
feature in Security Command
Center as a customizable volume

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:20.650
management tool.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

